{
  // Inherits the Nest build's `nodenext` module settings ON PURPOSE.
  //
  // The obvious move is to force `module: commonjs` for ts-jest's CommonJS
  // sandbox, but it is both unnecessary and harmful. Unnecessary: apps/api has
  // no `"type": "module"`, so under nodenext TypeScript already classifies every
  // .ts file here as CommonJS and emits require() — the same output nest build
  // produces. Harmful: `moduleResolution: node` cannot see package `exports`,
  // and `csv-parse/sync` exists ONLY as an export map entry, with no sync/
  // directory on disk. Jest's own resolver handles exports fine, so forcing it
  // would give an import that works at runtime and fails type checking.
  //
  // NOTE ON TYPE CHECKING — ts-jest does none of it, here or anywhere.
  // ts-jest 29 reads tsconfig's `isolatedModules` as its own transpile-only
  // switch (config-set.js:228 feeds ts-compiler.js:78, which then never builds a
  // LanguageService), and `nest new` ships `isolatedModules: true`. Setting it
  // false is not an escape: ts-jest then warns TS151002, because it cannot
  // type-check a hybrid `nodenext` module kind at all.
  //
  // So type checking is tsc's job, not the runner's: `npm run typecheck` runs
  // this config with --noEmit, and the test scripts depend on it. That is
  // strictly better than the runner doing it — one pass over the whole project
  // instead of per-file, and it covers files no test imports.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // Nothing is emitted; this config exists to be type-checked and to feed
    // ts-jest's transpiler.
    "noEmit": true,
    "declaration": false,
    "incremental": false,
    "sourceMap": false,
    "types": ["jest", "node"]
  }
}
